  <h1>India’s Cybersecurity Landscape — Threats and Policy Responses</h1>
  <div><em>Indian Express | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>As India becomes increasingly digital, cybersecurity has emerged as a national security and governance priority. In 2024, Indian Express reported a rise in cyberattacks on financial institutions, government portals, and critical infrastructure, underlining gaps in preparedness. For UPSC, cybersecurity links technology, governance, and security.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	Cybercrime Cases (NCRB 2023): 65,000+ reported (sharp rise).<br/>•	Financial Fraud: UPI and digital payment scams increasing.<br/>•	Critical Infrastructure Attacks: Power grids, hospitals, and govt sites targeted.<br/>•	Global Standing: India among top 3 countries facing cyberattacks (CERT-In).<br/>•	Legislation: IT Act (2000), CERT-In guidelines, draft Digital India Act (pending).<br/>________________________________________<br/>Key Threats<br/>1.	Financial Frauds: Phishing, ransomware, UPI scams.<br/>2.	Critical Infrastructure Risks: Power, transport, defence networks vulnerable.<br/>3.	Geopolitical Attacks: State-sponsored hackers (esp. from China, Pakistan).<br/>4.	Personal Data Breaches: Weak safeguards in public/private databases.<br/>5.	AI & Deepfake Risks: New frontier for misinformation.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Government Measures<br/>•	CERT-In (2004): Incident response and advisories.<br/>•	National Cyber Security Policy (2013): Yet to be updated.<br/>•	National Critical Information Infrastructure Protection Centre (NCIIPC): Secures key sectors.<br/>•	Cyber Surakshit Bharat (2018): Awareness campaign.<br/>•	Draft Digital India Act (2023): Comprehensive replacement for IT Act.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Achievements<br/>•	Digital India Growth: Cybersecurity now mainstream policy issue.<br/>•	CERT-In Alerts: Faster reporting and response mechanisms.<br/>•	International Cooperation: Cyber dialogues with US, EU, Japan.<br/>•	Awareness: Training programs for officials and corporates.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Persistent Gaps<br/>•	Outdated Policy: National Cyber Policy not revised since 2013.<br/>•	Skill Shortage: Deficit of cybersecurity professionals (~1 million gap).<br/>•	Fragmented Jurisdiction: Overlap between MeitY, MHA, CERT-In.<br/>•	Weak Citizen Awareness: Rural and elderly populations vulnerable.<br/>•	Data Protection Law: Passed in 2023 but enforcement still weak.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	National Security: Cyber warfare part of hybrid conflicts.<br/>•	Economic Stability: Secure digital payments vital for $5T economy goal.<br/>•	Social Trust: Citizens’ faith in digital governance depends on security.<br/>•	Global Standing: Cyber diplomacy crucial in India’s foreign policy.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-III (Security & Technology): Cybersecurity challenges.<br/>•	GS-II (Governance): Data protection, digital regulation.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Cybersecurity is the new frontier of national security.”<br/>o	“Digital empowerment without cybersecurity is a half-measure.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Cyber politics and global governance.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Update Cyber Policy: National Cybersecurity Strategy pending finalisation.<br/>2.	Skill Development: Train cybersecurity professionals at scale.<br/>3.	Strengthen CERT-In: Regional centres, faster response times.<br/>4.	Public Awareness: Digital hygiene campaigns for citizens.<br/>5.	Global Partnerships: Enhance cyber diplomacy for intelligence sharing.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s cybersecurity preparedness is at a critical juncture — progress has been made, but outdated frameworks and weak capacity leave gaps. Building a resilient, secure, and inclusive cyber ecosystem is vital. For UPSC aspirants, this issue reflects the convergence of governance, technology, and security.</p>
